    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/4/94151.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Tariffs as a threat to try to make countries treat us well, I think can be an effective tool. You can't overuse it, or I should say you should not overuse it. Number two, tariffs is kind of a retaliatory measure. A country is hitting us with 30% tariffs. Our tariffs are only 10. Well, maybe we need to make our tariffs 30. Just send the message that's what good for the goose is good for the gander. I'm fine with that. The third bucket of tariffs, which is kind of permanent, longstanding tariffs, those are actually a break on economic growth. Those are things that can actually make it harder for America to be prosperous. There are maybe some key industries where we want to build domestic capacity, think health care food, where maybe we want a little bit more of a protectionist approach. But I think the idea that everything Americans make or use needs to be made in America and that we're simply not going to export things elsewhere, that's probably not that is certainly not an America's interest. So I do think some tariffs can be real good. Others can be more problematic.",
